WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.707
[men screaming]

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.111
Elisa Au: Yeah, I would actually say

00:00:05.111 --> 00:00:07.194
this is about 100% accurate.

00:00:07.194 --> 00:00:09.530
At this level, this is what it looks like.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:11.070
Hi, my name is Elisa Au.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:14.450
I am a three-time WKF
karate world champion

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.853
and one of the few females here in the US

00:00:16.853 --> 00:00:18.930
poised to represent the US

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.610
at the Olympic games next summer in Tokyo.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:25.810
Today, I'll be watching a few
clips from martial arts movies

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.713
and letting you know how
real I think they are.

00:00:32.611 --> 00:00:37.611
[fighters grunting]
[blows thudding]

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.760
The moves you're seeing here, I mean,

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.700
they're not exactly karate moves.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.290
They're really close-range
type of techniques.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.400
He's just really just trying
to break the guy down,

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.980
more like boxing.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.980
In karate, we're using more
mid- to long-range techniques.

00:00:54.077 --> 00:00:55.910
[blows thudding]

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:57.553
See how he's just standing there?

00:00:57.553 --> 00:00:59.628
I mean, he's gotta defend that kick

00:00:59.628 --> 00:01:00.986
or get out of the way.

00:01:00.986 --> 00:01:02.795
These guys are just kind of standing there

00:01:02.795 --> 00:01:05.210
as kicking bags for one another.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:06.593
There's not a whole lot of

00:01:06.593 --> 00:01:09.251
defensive or distancing going on,

00:01:09.251 --> 00:01:11.980
but the attacks themselves
are pretty impressive

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.621
and very much martial arts moves.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:19.856
The fact that there's a referee

00:01:19.856 --> 00:01:22.306
and they didn't do anything,
that's kind of silly.

00:01:22.306 --> 00:01:23.540
Like, why is the referee there

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.850
if he's not gonna call poor sportsmanship?

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.626
So, that's unrealistic.

00:01:28.626 --> 00:01:32.070
[upbeat music]
[man grunting]

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.547
Jump spinning hook kick,
definitely a thing.

00:01:35.547 --> 00:01:37.320
And he does it so well.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.640
But the spinning hook kick

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.100
is more and more common nowadays

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.493
as the athleticism is going
up in karate, sport karate.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.840
Obviously, Jean-Claude Van
Damme has really nice kicks

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.060
that look great on screen, but, yeah,

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.554
it's not really that realistic,

00:01:54.554 --> 00:01:56.700
and the distance is not really there.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.121
For this one, I'd probably give it a six.

00:01:59.121 --> 00:02:00.833
Referee: And fight!

00:02:00.833 --> 00:02:04.475
[tense music]
[blows thudding]

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:11.936
Elisa: OK, so, as dramatized as this is,

00:02:11.936 --> 00:02:14.111
and kind of silly to a point,

00:02:14.111 --> 00:02:16.790
it is actually kind of
close to what you would see

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:20.405
in sport karate, like competition karate.

00:02:23.247 --> 00:02:24.820
See how they're just kind
of posing at each other

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.240
in a very combative stance?

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.450
That doesn't happen in sport karate.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.960
So, we stand in a very neutral stance.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:33.600
I mean, if I were to face somebody

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.210
and they tried to do this at the line,

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.390
I would shake my head [laughs]

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.300
because it's not OK.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:41.930
Like, you need to start neutral,

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:43.760
and once the referee calls to start,

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.996
then you can get into your stance.

00:02:45.996 --> 00:02:50.996
[blows thudding]
[upbeat music]

00:02:55.003 --> 00:02:57.200
I actually really liked that sequence.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.710
There were a lot more hook punches

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.300
than would happen in karate.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.640
Karate punches in sport are very straight.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.090
There are no hook punches
or uppercuts allowed,

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.650
but I liked that there was,
like, a volley back and forth

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:11.480
and there was some sort
of usage of distance,

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.410
which is much more typical in karate.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:15.030
And I saw some blocking there.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.410
So that's actually really realistic,

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:20.768
just without all the hook punches.

00:03:20.768 --> 00:03:25.768
[blows thudding]
[fighters grunting]

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:30.140
So, that sequence started
out pretty realistic

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:32.010
with the punching and the blocking,

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:33.840
but then he started adding elbows in,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.400
which are totally not
allowed in competition.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.463
But I like the punches and the blocks.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:43.930
And those sweeps are pretty
cool, and they do happen,

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.805
but they don't happen very often.

00:03:45.805 --> 00:03:49.303
Yeah, and I guess the one
thing that's missing here

00:03:49.303 --> 00:03:52.517
is the lack of protective equipment.

00:03:52.517 --> 00:03:53.891
We're way suited up

00:03:53.891 --> 00:03:56.383
compared to any of these
guys on these scenes.

00:03:56.383 --> 00:03:57.730
Like, I'm wearing fist
guards, shin guards,

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.670
insteps, mouth guard,

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.110
a chest protector, body protector.

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:03.470
I mean, I'm, like, totally suited up

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.040
when I go in for competition.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.210
In sport karate or Olympic-style karate,

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:08.860
we don't wear the head protection;

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:11.070
however, some of the kids under 18

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:12.768
actually wear head protection.

00:04:12.768 --> 00:04:14.001
Yeah...[sighs]

00:04:14.001 --> 00:04:15.984
I mean, I give this one a seven,

00:04:15.984 --> 00:04:17.567
only 'cause it's better,

00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:19.890
it's more realistic than "Bloodsport"

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:22.640
compared to what happens
in real competitions,

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.784
but it's also really goofy.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:30.142
[upbeat music]
[fighters grunting]

00:04:33.916 --> 00:04:36.840
Yeah, so, I mean, obviously
we're looking at a street fight.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:38.350
These guys are using self-defense,

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.150
but they have that karate background.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:44.260
They are pretty much sticking
to karate techniques,

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.730
which is actually kind of nice,

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.039
even though the guys attacking them

00:04:47.039 --> 00:04:49.328
are attacking them with
chains and stuff like that.

00:04:49.328 --> 00:04:52.540
Another thing also to mention
when it comes to self-defense

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:54.570
is that you don't want to be on one leg.

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:56.960
Any kicks where they've
got their leg up in the air

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.840
for too long, that's
not a good place to be

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.260
when you're trying to defend yourself.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.820
It's just not good for your balance.

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:03.640
Your vitals are exposed.

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:08.540
So it's definitely for the
scene to make it more flashy.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:11.640
You wouldn't see, in real self-defense,

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.363
people using that many kicks.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.660
Yeah. See, it's so many
flying kicks, it's crazy.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:19.380
Yep. So that's all good.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.380
I like all that.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.240
Definitely using, like,
wrist grabs and stuff.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.720
Yeah, throws are
definitely part of karate.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.220
Different martial arts
weren't developed in a silo.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:32.310
A lot of the originators
of the martial arts

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:34.378
actually trained together

00:05:34.378 --> 00:05:36.640
or cross-trained in different disciplines,

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:39.280
so there's a lot of
crossover when it comes

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:41.810
to the actual techniques in martial arts.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:43.990
So although you might think, for example,

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:46.000
that throws are a judo thing,

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.500
there's definitely
throws in karate as well.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.850
What we can use in competition,
that's a different story,

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:53.580
because we're not allowed
to do the types of grabs.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.360
So there's certain things we can do,

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:57.440
like if someone kicks us, like I said,

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:59.840
we can grab quickly and
go for the takedown.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.960
You can't do, like, those big throws

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:03.230
that you see in judo that are, like,

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:06.013
over the shoulder or over
the hip. That doesn't happen.

00:06:09.044 --> 00:06:11.010
[body thuds]

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.060
Yeah, yeah. So, right there,

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:13.768
he's, like, totally back to the guy.

00:06:13.768 --> 00:06:16.222
The guy should've just hit him. [laughing]

00:06:16.222 --> 00:06:18.200
What was that?

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:19.563
A lot of these movies,

00:06:19.563 --> 00:06:21.080
they're spinning when they're just, like,

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.213
an arm's length from their opponent.

00:06:23.213 --> 00:06:24.423
Why would you do that?

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:27.441
If I were just to rate it on

00:06:27.441 --> 00:06:29.499
just the pure karate
moves that I'm seeing,

00:06:29.499 --> 00:06:31.550
I'd actually give it more
like a seven or eight,

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:33.970
just 'cause there was actually
a lot of pretty good moves

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:35.503
in there, even though
it wasn't a competition.

00:06:35.503 --> 00:06:36.678
But at the end,

00:06:36.678 --> 00:06:40.628
with the little spin
kick to finish it off,

00:06:40.628 --> 00:06:42.120
that's a little unrealistic.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.256
[crowd chattering]

00:06:46.256 --> 00:06:48.506
[hits thudding]
[grunting]

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:51.950
He's keeping the guy away.

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:55.160
He obviously knows that he's
up against kung fu fighters

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:57.420
who probably would be really good

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.520
in close-combat competition.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:03.010
So, karate, one of its
strengths as a martial art

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:04.793
is that it has really good

00:07:04.793 --> 00:07:06.534
medium- to large-distance moves,

00:07:06.534 --> 00:07:10.130
like the big side kicks, the
punches with full extension.

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:13.170
That's kind of the hallmark
movements of karate

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:16.372
that gives it its strength

00:07:16.372 --> 00:07:18.230
when up against other martial arts.

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:21.320
For him to keep the other
guy away using side kicks

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:24.017
and other larger karate moves
is actually really smart.

00:07:24.017 --> 00:07:26.200
If I were in his place, I'd
probably do something like that,

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:28.700
knowing that once we
got in that closer range

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:30.730
that I'd be at a disadvantage

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:33.073
when up against a kung fu fighter.

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:38.290
So, actually, we don't use karate chops.

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:41.253
That's one of those
things that everyone says,

00:07:41.253 --> 00:07:42.473
but it's not really a thing.

00:07:42.473 --> 00:07:45.380
I mean, there are open-hand
techniques in karate,

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.170
but it's not that, like, "hi-ya!"

00:07:47.170 --> 00:07:50.386
that people constantly think

00:07:50.386 --> 00:07:53.495
is what karate is all about.

00:07:53.495 --> 00:07:55.253
[fighter yelling]

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:00.560
Yeah, see, again, the
running and the jumping,

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.719
I mean, the kung fu guy saw it.

00:08:02.719 --> 00:08:05.140
You know? [laughs] No
one's gonna stand there

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:07.030
as you run and jump at them.

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:10.890
There's one thing that none
of these scenes really do

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:14.150
that sport karate has kind of evolved to,

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:15.642
is that there's a lot more, like,

00:08:15.642 --> 00:08:17.450
bouncing and movement going on.

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:19.470
So when you see us fighting, at first,

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:21.080
you'd be like, "They look
like they're jumping beans."

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:22.760
But then when you watch
a little more carefully,

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:24.870
it's not so much jumping up and down,

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:27.370
but we're very light on our toes.

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:29.080
Always juking, always faking.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:30.600
When you get to the higher level,

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:33.100
it's always a game of distance and timing.

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:34.760
Everyone's techniques are pretty good.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:36.180
There's that chess element

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:38.883
of thinking a couple moves ahead,

00:08:38.883 --> 00:08:41.502
and one of the sports I think
it's actually a lot like

00:08:41.502 --> 00:08:42.720
is fencing.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:44.642
In fencing, they're
constantly trying to, like,

00:08:44.642 --> 00:08:45.758
touch and not be touched,

00:08:45.758 --> 00:08:48.670
and that's really similar to
what we're doing in karate.

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:51.550
But it's that idea of,
like, knowing your distance

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.240
and trying to touch or score a point

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.267
before the other person
can score a point on you.

00:08:56.267 --> 00:08:59.017
[blows thudding]

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.993
Yeah, he's done. Kung fu wins today.

00:09:04.993 --> 00:09:06.890
[laughs]

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:08.460
He doesn't even fall correctly.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:11.700
I mean, that's, like, martial arts 101.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.240
Don't put your wrist down like that.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:15.270
You're gonna break your wrist.

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:17.400
You gotta fall softly.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:21.010
I would give this one
more of a four or five.

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:22.960
I can't speak for the kung fu side of it,

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:25.046
but for the karate side of it, I mean,

00:09:25.046 --> 00:09:26.780
he started out OK,

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:29.103
and then it kind of just
went downhill from there.

00:09:29.103 --> 00:09:30.460
Ira: First person to
three wins, all right?

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:31.810
Dwight: Yes, sensei.
Michael: A lot of rules,

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:32.643
a lot of rules.

00:09:32.643 --> 00:09:33.880
On the street, we didn't have any rules.

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:37.500
Maybe one. No kicks to the
groin, home for dinner.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.190
Elisa: Yeah, so kicks below
the belt in competition

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:42.440
aren't allowed, for obvious reasons.

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:44.180
We just don't kick legs or anything.

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:45.990
You can score kicks to the body,

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:48.630
the back, and then any part of the face,

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:50.487
depending on your age, with control.

00:09:50.487 --> 00:09:51.754
Ira: Hajime!
[Dwight yelling]

00:09:51.754 --> 00:09:54.540
Michael: Whoa, hey,
what the hell was that?

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:55.670
Elisa: It was a kick to the back.

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:58.240
It counted. It's good.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:00.914
See, this is typical at this level.

00:10:00.914 --> 00:10:03.130
You know, he's a purple
belt, so it's like novice.

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:03.963
Michael: Idiot.

00:10:03.963 --> 00:10:07.710
[fighters yelling]

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:10.540
Elisa: So, a lot of punches
and kicks to the air

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:13.070
because they just don't
understand their distancing yet.

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:15.340
So that's really common
for a beginner level.

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:18.320
You don't want to be running
away, like how he did it.

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:20.350
But distance is really
important in karate.

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:21.750
When you start getting to a higher level,

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:23.490
and once you realize
you're kind of in trouble,

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:24.450
you're in the danger zone,

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:27.635
you do have strategies and
tactics for getting out,

00:10:27.635 --> 00:10:30.312
not running away like he's doing it.

00:10:30.312 --> 00:10:32.397
They must have had so
much fun filming this.

00:10:32.397 --> 00:10:33.413
Michael: One point, two
points, three points!

00:10:33.413 --> 00:10:36.960
Elisa: So, you can't accumulate points

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:38.700
with, like, a combination of punches.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.520
It's just one point for one technique.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.890
And now the sensei should actually stop,

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:45.453
because this is not like karate.

00:10:45.453 --> 00:10:47.115
He needs to separate them

00:10:47.115 --> 00:10:48.780
and let them sort themselves out,

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:50.170
because now they're gonna start wrestling,

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:52.480
and that's when people get injured.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:53.650
And that's the whole point of the referee,

00:10:53.650 --> 00:10:55.923
is to stop people from getting injured.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:59.680
OK, so we're not doing karate
anymore, obviously. [laughs]

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:02.810
I would actually say this
is about 100% accurate,

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:03.830
at this level.

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:06.420
Especially, like, adults,
when they start fighting

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.130
and they don't have a lot of experience,

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:10.910
they kind of have that,
like, almost scared to go in.

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:13.870
I mean, honestly, I would give this one

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:17.063
a 10 in terms of realism, at this level.

00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:23.930
That was a solid punch to the face,

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:28.130
and this is non-contact, or
it's not full-contact karate.

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:31.500
So I think it's a little
excessive, especially for,

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:34.470
they're supposed to be
high-school kids in this movie.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:37.500
Again, no gear. They're in high school.

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:39.631
Seems really dangerous, but OK.

00:11:41.590 --> 00:11:42.920
OK, here we go.

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:45.500
The famous move, right?

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:47.647
I mean, yeah, it wouldn't happen.

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:50.490
If someone was doing that
across the way from me,

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:52.830
I would not go straight at
him, like on a straight line.

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:54.427
That's for sure.

00:11:54.427 --> 00:11:56.970
Yeah, I mean, this is just
not a real karate move.

00:11:56.970 --> 00:12:00.770
Let's just get that
straight right off the bat.

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:04.775
In a tournament like this,

00:12:04.775 --> 00:12:05.983
you're not gonna be doing that.

00:12:05.983 --> 00:12:08.610
If you're that injured, if
your leg is that injured,

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:12.105
you're not gonna kick with it.

00:12:12.105 --> 00:12:16.390
It's not the right move to use.

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:18.860
It's really made for the movies.

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:19.996
This is not something

00:12:19.996 --> 00:12:22.263
that you would see someone
do in a tournament.

00:12:22.263 --> 00:12:27.130
If I were down or needed
to actually get a point

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:29.070
in the last few seconds and I was hurt,

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:30.480
I would go for a punch.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:32.470
I wouldn't take the
risk of going for a kick

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:34.870
when I only need to get
one point with a punch.

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:37.970
So I'd probably go for
some, like, big punch

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:40.005
to try to get that last point.

00:12:40.005 --> 00:12:41.500
The new "Karate Kid," "Cobra Kai,"

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:44.410
is actually a tiny bit
more realistic than this.

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:46.973
So I have to give this one a six.

00:12:46.973 --> 00:12:48.290
But this is just classic.

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:50.270
I mean, me and my teammates are always

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:52.203
making references to "The Karate Kid."

00:12:52.203 --> 00:12:54.270
But not realistic.

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:00.992
Obviously, I think they're trying

00:13:00.992 --> 00:13:03.200
to recreate that bobbing motion

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:05.970
that you saw in the video games
when you played it as a kid,

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:09.960
but we don't do it so
dramatically as you see it here.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:12.020
But there is, like,
this bouncing movement.

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:13.410
It's always a distance game.

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:15.750
Like, I take a step in,
and I take a step out.

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:18.480
This one is obviously trying
to copy the video game,

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:21.430
where the people are
completely side-facing,

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:22.850
which is also not a karate thing.

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:24.173
Like, you don't turn your back,

00:13:24.173 --> 00:13:27.590
because in karate, the
back is a target area.

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:28.848
So you're exposing something

00:13:28.848 --> 00:13:31.080
you would have a very hard time blocking.

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.921
And, yeah, just the stance itself

00:13:33.921 --> 00:13:35.956
is not realistic for karate.

00:13:35.956 --> 00:13:38.750
[blows thudding]

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:41.480
If you were to really use
a hook kick or a heel kick,

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:42.863
you would use your heel

00:13:42.863 --> 00:13:44.271
to hit someone in, like, the temple.

00:13:44.271 --> 00:13:45.698
That would be ideal, right, to hit someone

00:13:45.698 --> 00:13:46.774
in the temple with your heel.

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:51.420
No, he's just being really flashy.

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:53.932
Like, a lot of tae kwon do practitioners

00:13:53.932 --> 00:13:57.310
can do that kind of stuff,
which is really cool.

00:13:57.310 --> 00:13:58.810
And it's also confusing, right?

00:13:58.810 --> 00:13:59.710
Someone's spinning at you,

00:13:59.710 --> 00:14:01.630
you're kind of like, whoa, what the heck?

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:04.180
But it also creates a lot of momentum.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:06.600
So it's really good for,
like, a knockout type of blow,

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.010
which is not what you're doing in karate.

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:11.440
So it's not as typical.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:14.465
Oh, I give this one maybe a six or seven.

00:14:14.465 --> 00:14:15.781
I mean, yeah.

00:14:18.973 --> 00:14:20.780
OK, so, see how they're
standing like that,

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:23.498
with, not feet together, but
when their feet are apart?

00:14:23.498 --> 00:14:24.625
That's like the kamae.

00:14:24.625 --> 00:14:28.360
That's how we actually
start karate matches.

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:30.160
It's a neutral position.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:33.040
It's kind of like a stance of respect

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:34.800
rather than aggression.

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:36.140
So this is totally realistic.

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:37.525
And it's funny because, like,

00:14:37.525 --> 00:14:41.100
obviously this guy came to
this dojo to beat this guy up,

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:43.120
but I'm gonna show respect anyway

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:44.930
at the beginning of the match.

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:46.830
So, they don't start in their position,

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:48.150
they just start neutral.

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:49.890
And I think a lot of that
really comes from, like,

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:53.910
samurai formalities
before the actual battle.

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:56.803
And so some of that spilled
into karate, for sure.

00:14:58.426 --> 00:15:03.426
[blows thudding]
[fighters grunting]

00:15:05.180 --> 00:15:06.920
Yeah, see that punch?

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:09.360
That is hallmark karate right there.

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:11.760
So, what the guy in the white gi did

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:13.637
is that as the black guy gi

00:15:13.637 --> 00:15:16.302
made a small mistake of
getting in too close,

00:15:16.302 --> 00:15:19.760
the white-gi guy was right
there for the reverse punch.

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:23.310
So, the reverse punch is like karate 101.

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:26.630
It's learning how to throw
a good reverse punch,

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:28.098
and it's a technique you use

00:15:28.098 --> 00:15:30.020
all the way up until the elite level.

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:32.770
It's just, it's super strong.

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:34.640
So, he timed it perfectly.

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:37.210
Black-gi guy was coming
in at the wrong distance,

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:39.000
and he caught him right in the stomach.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:42.272
The jab is the lead hand, the
front hand, the kizami zuki.

00:15:42.272 --> 00:15:44.722
And then the back hand
is the reverse punch.

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:48.010
Ooh, nice combination.

00:15:48.010 --> 00:15:50.140
I don't know if that could be executed

00:15:50.140 --> 00:15:53.050
unless you're really,
really good. [laughs]

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:55.502
But that was really
cool though. I liked it.

00:15:55.502 --> 00:15:56.592
[fighter yelling]

00:15:56.592 --> 00:15:58.378
[kick thuds]
Fighter: Kiai!

00:15:58.378 --> 00:15:59.336
Elisa: And this is another thing

00:15:59.336 --> 00:16:00.560
that we haven't seen too much

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:03.243
in the other clips, is this "kiai,"

00:16:03.243 --> 00:16:05.710
so that yell that you hear.

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:08.720
And this is a hallmark of karate.

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:10.970
Sometimes other sports,
like in tae kwon do,

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:13.980
they'll be like, "Why are
you guys yelling so much?

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:15.810
Why all the noise?"

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:18.300
It's important, because kiai in karate,

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:20.023
it, like, brings out your spirit.

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:22.820
It's like that extra oomph

00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:25.965
to make the technique effective.

00:16:25.965 --> 00:16:26.831
Kiai!

00:16:26.831 --> 00:16:29.700
The kiai is a big part
of even what we do now

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:31.440
in modern-day sport karate.

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:33.025
If you don't kiai, you don't get a point.

00:16:33.025 --> 00:16:35.869
You've gotta kiai if
you want to get a point.

00:16:35.869 --> 00:16:37.920
Yeah, I mean, it's not what I do.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:39.861
This is definitely more of, like,

00:16:39.861 --> 00:16:41.640
karate from back in the day,

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:43.306
but having said that,

00:16:43.306 --> 00:16:44.739
I'd say it's pretty much a 10.

00:16:44.739 --> 00:16:47.622
I mean, this is what karate looked like.

00:16:47.622 --> 00:16:49.154
Fighter: Give me your best shot.

00:16:49.154 --> 00:16:51.654
[tense music]

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:55.810
Elisa: She's using her distance correctly,

00:16:55.810 --> 00:16:57.460
so I guess that's good.

00:16:57.460 --> 00:16:59.680
You know, she's obviously a weaker,

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:01.200
smaller opponent than him,

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:03.750
so to be more agile and to move more

00:17:03.750 --> 00:17:07.043
and stay out of his range
is a smart thing to do.

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:13.922
There's just far too
many kicks in this scene,

00:17:13.922 --> 00:17:16.135
and her hands are not even up

00:17:16.135 --> 00:17:20.250
even to, like, even try protect herself.

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:22.743
It's pretty unrealistic that she's just

00:17:22.743 --> 00:17:24.910
kicking her way through
beating this guy up.

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:31.010
First of all, it's more like
tae kwon do if anything,

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:32.850
because she's only kicking.

00:17:32.850 --> 00:17:36.100
Just the way she's just
using mostly her front leg,

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:37.700
just as, like, to keep him away

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:39.910
and almost jab at him with her front leg,

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:41.760
that's very much more
like a tae kwon do thing

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:42.763
than a karate thing.

00:17:45.603 --> 00:17:46.436
Julie: Kiai!

00:17:46.436 --> 00:17:47.720
Elisa: And did you hear her kiai?

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:49.966
You can tell she's not a martial artist.

00:17:49.966 --> 00:17:51.432
She just went like, "ke-yuh."

00:17:51.432 --> 00:17:52.265
Julie: Kiai!

00:17:52.265 --> 00:17:53.423
Elisa: [laughing] It's just like,

00:17:53.423 --> 00:17:55.591
no one kiais like that.

00:17:55.591 --> 00:17:57.300
When you kiai, you kiai for a reason.

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:59.270
It's like, if she really
wanted to hurt him,

00:17:59.270 --> 00:18:00.340
she'd be like, "Kiai!"

00:18:00.340 --> 00:18:02.640
Like, she would put
something into that sound.

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:03.650
But she's just saying it

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:07.030
like she's just imitating
what she's probably seen.

00:18:07.030 --> 00:18:08.920
And she's just going, "Huh." You know?

00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:10.688
And this guy should figure out, like,

00:18:10.688 --> 00:18:14.613
maybe one kick she'll get in on this guy.

00:18:14.613 --> 00:18:15.579
Not 10.

00:18:15.579 --> 00:18:17.300
Like, he's just standing there

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:20.512
and taking it like a punching bag.

00:18:20.512 --> 00:18:23.420
Yeah, I'm gonna give
this one, like, a four.

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:24.353
And that's generous.

00:18:24.353 --> 00:18:27.713
But I'm not a fan of this one, no.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:38.893
Again, this is not a competition.

00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:43.400
They're in a real-life
situation in this scene.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:46.480
But the one thing I do
see that's pretty cool

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:48.307
is that they're kind
of, like, using distance

00:18:48.307 --> 00:18:50.370
and, like, just trying
to see who's gonna flinch

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:52.295
and who's gonna make the move first.

00:18:57.410 --> 00:18:58.730
So, the front kick's good.

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:00.220
But then the jumping stuff,

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:03.010
like, that's just for
flair, because, like, why?

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:06.410
Why would you use all
that energy to do that?

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:08.620
You're already catching
him with regular kicks.

00:19:08.620 --> 00:19:10.273
Why the jumping kicks?

00:19:12.614 --> 00:19:14.530
I mean, it's not realistic
that he's wearing a suit

00:19:14.530 --> 00:19:17.290
and he can do this. [laughs]

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:19.898
He's gotta have, like, stretch pants on.

00:19:21.994 --> 00:19:24.585
He's definitely using
distance more than I've seen

00:19:24.585 --> 00:19:26.240
in the other scenes.

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:27.800
They're not just running at each other

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:29.491
and doing big old attacks.

00:19:31.141 --> 00:19:32.457
That was pretty cool.

00:19:32.457 --> 00:19:34.743
I mean, it's good. It's real karate.

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:38.100
See, like that. That roundhouse kick,

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:41.170
that's a real karate technique
that's super effective.

00:19:41.170 --> 00:19:44.140
Like, you catch someone
with a solid roundhouse kick

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:46.670
or a solid side kick,
that's what you need.

00:19:46.670 --> 00:19:48.650
But really, when you see
those kind of techniques,

00:19:48.650 --> 00:19:50.540
that's what makes a
martial artist go, "Yeah!"

00:19:50.540 --> 00:19:52.810
Like, that was a nice roundhouse kick.

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:55.140
And I don't know that other
people watching the movie

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:57.010
would have been as excited about that.

00:19:57.010 --> 00:19:58.195
They probably care more about

00:19:58.195 --> 00:20:00.620
the finishing kick at the end.

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:04.845
But these are the real
techniques that I like to see.

00:20:04.845 --> 00:20:06.703
Yeah, I mean, I liked the distancing

00:20:06.703 --> 00:20:07.737
that they did in the beginning,

00:20:07.737 --> 00:20:09.350
and there was some
other really cool stuff,

00:20:09.350 --> 00:20:13.125
but it was also super flashy,

00:20:13.125 --> 00:20:14.310
because it's for a movie.

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:15.985
So, I don't know,

00:20:15.985 --> 00:20:17.509
I'd give it, like, a seven.

00:20:17.509 --> 00:20:20.256
[blows thudding]

00:20:25.053 --> 00:20:27.288
Yeah, so this girl
obviously has more of like

00:20:27.288 --> 00:20:30.840
a tae kwon do or acrobatic background.

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:32.473
This is not karate at all.

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:34.798
This whole scene is just her

00:20:34.798 --> 00:20:36.214
jumping off of a bunch of people

00:20:36.214 --> 00:20:38.283
who are like her springboard.

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:41.850
It's not really showing any
kind of karate technique.

00:20:43.225 --> 00:20:44.350
Like, it's just not...

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:47.591
a lot of walking on people.

00:20:47.591 --> 00:20:48.982
But it looks really cool.

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:53.689
She doesn't know how to throw
punches properly, either.

00:20:53.689 --> 00:20:56.573
Like, that one punch she
threw, out of all the punches,

00:20:56.573 --> 00:20:58.448
she kind of just threw her hand out there,

00:20:58.448 --> 00:21:00.210
whereas her kicks look like

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:02.303
she actually knows what she's doing.

00:21:04.424 --> 00:21:06.710
So, the guy comes up to her and he yells,

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:08.460
and he just stands there
and just waits for her

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:10.210
to complete her spinning back kick.

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:13.690
Like, they're not doing anything.

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:15.370
It's not real.

00:21:15.370 --> 00:21:17.420
See, they're not throwing punches either.

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:20.555
No one's throwing any
techniques at this girl.

00:21:20.555 --> 00:21:21.803
[laughing] And this one's spot running.

00:21:21.803 --> 00:21:24.690
Oh, and he comes at with her
with his stomach exposed.

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:27.533
Like, he doesn't even
block his own midsection.

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:32.450
This is terrible, in my opinion. [laughs]

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:34.000
Oh, and he's just standing there.

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:35.440
Like, that kid was just sitting there

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:38.365
waiting to be a stepping stool, so.

00:21:38.365 --> 00:21:41.502
Yeah, I'd probably give this one a two.

00:21:41.502 --> 00:21:43.592
I mean,

00:21:43.592 --> 00:21:45.692
she's throwing legit kicks,

00:21:45.692 --> 00:21:47.150
but, I mean, there's no distance.

00:21:47.150 --> 00:21:48.258
Everyone's just standing there

00:21:48.258 --> 00:21:50.610
and just waiting for her to kick them.

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:52.433
You know, it's just absurd.

